= Electric current — Simple =

'''Electric current''' measures how much electric charge passes a point in a circuit each second.

It is measured in amperes (amps). One ampere means one coulomb of charge passes a chosen surface every second.

In a metal wire, electrons move and carry the charge. Engineers usually draw the direction of current as the direction positive charges would move, which is opposite to the electrons' drift in that wire.

A [[Resistor|resistor]] can restrict how much current flows for a given voltage.

